{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 55528ae4: G3's unknown staging write.\n\nI accept your reading. No act at Destination 18 settles a recorded\nunknown stage from a later ref read, and changing a pinned definition\nis out. The witness room stays as it is. Send nothing more to it.\n\nFind the cause first. Every Git write on the mini has recorded\nunknown: the first-head writes and this stage. Each was then confirmed\nor found present by a later read. That pattern points at the transport,\nnot at chance. celld 0.6.3 already has a known stream fault, from the\n2026-10-27 validation (\"head stream fails on celld 0.6.3, RPC streams\nacross isolates\"). So the receive-pack reply probably never reaches\nsendOnce.\n\nI file the diagnosis as its own request. Its result decides the rest:\n\n1. If a transport fix in dap, or a celld setting, delivers the reply,\n   then G3 finishes in a fresh room on the same cohort. The room binds\n   to a new empty branch in the same repository. main there already\n   holds dee19e0b and the reservation ref.\n2. If no fix in dap can deliver the reply, the stage-reconciliation\n   operation you describe becomes a design request for the next\n   Destination version, with its own review, after OAuth O3.\n\nYour design rules for reconciliation stand for either path. It names\nthe publication and its exact reservation, re-reads the ref through\nthe trusted host port, records that observation apart from the\nunknown send, and then continues through the ordinary checks. A\nmissing, mismatched or unavailable ref never advances it. Durable\nreceive-pack replies help future crash recovery, but they do not\nsettle this publication.\n\nOrder: the diagnosis comes after O2 reaches review, ahead of O3. G3\nacceptance stays open.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}